    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d68">

                <pb facs="0151" n="115" sameAs="frus:frus1958-60v07p2/pg115"/>

                <head>68. Letter From the British Ambassador (Caccia) to Secretary of State
                        Dulles<anchor n="0" xml:id="d68-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1958-11-07">November 7, 1958</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: The Foreign Secretary has
                    asked me to let you have for your personal information the attached account of
                    his conversation with M. Couve de Murville in London yesterday about General de
                    Gaulle’s ideas on tripartite co-operation.</p>
                <p>If you could manage it before leaving for Seattle,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d68-f2"/>
                    I should be most grateful for an opportunity of a word about this. We have had
                    other reports of the General’s views from other sources, which I should also
                    like to mention.</p>
                <p>Yours sincerely,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Harold Caccia</hi></signed></closer>



                <xi:include href="d68-a1.xml" xpointer="d68-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d68-f1" n="0" type="source" xml:id="d68-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Presidential Correspondence: Lot 66 D 204. Secret;
                    Personal. a handwritten notation on the source text reads: “Sec saw.”</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d68-f2" n="1" xml:id="d68-f2-content">
                <p>Dulles attended the Colombo Plan Meeting in Seattle November 10–13.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
